# PART A — OVERALL PLATFORM STRUCTURE

## 1. Introduce two distinct community environments

Build Buds should have two related but independently controlled environments.

### Environment 1: Public Community

This is the public-facing community discovery experience.

Anyone should be able to open the website and browse published content without signing in.

Visitors should be able to:

- Discover public posts.
- Explore games and websites created by young builders.
- Read public announcements.
- Discover new digital tools and creative projects.
- Read tutorials, discussions and articles.
- Explore project screenshots and previews.
- View comments and public replies.
- Browse categories and discover popular content.

However, visitors must authenticate with Google before performing actions such as:

- Reacting to a post.
- Commenting.
- Replying to comments.
- Creating a post.
- Publishing a project.
- Bookmarking content.
- Participating in other interactive public-community activities.

Public account authentication must not automatically grant access to the private student chat.

### Environment 2: Private Student Community

This is the existing Discord-style Community Hub.

It contains the established:

- General Chat.
- Announcements.
- Updates.
- Help.
- Project channels.
- Existing community conversations.
- Member presence.
- Message threads.
- Reactions.
- Existing messaging interactions.
- Other current Community Hub functionality.

This environment must only be available to students whose registration under Jaystarbliss Studios has been verified and approved by an authorised administrator.

Parents, ordinary website visitors and unverified public accounts must not gain access.

### Absolute separation requirement

Public posts and private chat messages are separate systems.

Never expose private General Chat messages, private Updates, internal announcements, student-only resource links or private channel histories in the public feed.

Do not copy private chat content into public posts automatically.

The public community can display announcements deliberately published for public viewing, but private Community Hub content must remain private.

