# PART I — FIREBASE AND DATABASE SECURITY

## 10. Preserve and strengthen the existing Firebase architecture

Firebase Authentication and Firestore must remain the authoritative backend.

Inspect the existing Firestore collections and security rules before introducing new collections.

Do not create parallel collections that duplicate existing user profiles or current chat data unnecessarily.

### Suggested data structures

Adapt these to the existing schema rather than blindly duplicating fields.

**Public user profiles**

`users/{uid}`

Store basic profile information and public account status.

**Private user information**

`users_private/{uid}`

Keep private email and sensitive account-related information separate.

**Public posts**

`publicPosts/{postId}`

Store post title, body, author, category, tags, image metadata, published status and timestamps.

**Public comments**

`publicPosts/{postId}/comments/{commentId}`

Store comments and optional parent/reply references.

**Public reactions**

`publicPosts/{postId}/reactions/{uid}`

Use the authenticated UID to prevent duplicated reactions.

**Student access requests**

`studentAccessRequests/{uid}`

Store the applicant's private student-registration details and review status.

Use the authenticated Firebase UID as the application identity to prevent multiple competing requests from the same account.

**Trusted student memberships**

`communityMemberships/{uid}`

Store approved membership status and relevant audit information.

**User notifications**

`userNotifications/{uid}/items/{notificationId}`

Store internal Build Buds notification records and read status.

**Administrative audit records**

`adminAuditLogs/{logId}`

Record sensitive administrative decisions.

Do not expose private student verification information through public profile queries.

### Firestore Security Rules

This is one of the most important parts of the implementation.

Review and update the actual deployed Firestore security rules.

Do not rely on frontend conditionals to protect data.

Implement the following access model:

| Resource | Public visitor | Signed-in public user | Approved student | Admin |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Published public posts | Read | Read | Read | Manage |
| Public comments | Read | Read/write as permitted | Read/write | Manage |
| Public reactions | Read | Read/write | Read/write | Manage |
| Student access requests | No | Own request only | Own request/status | Review |
| Private chat | No | No | Yes, subject to channel permissions | Yes |
| Private chat attachments | No | No | Existing permissions | Existing permissions |
| Admin dashboard | No | No | No | Yes |
| Other students' private records | No | No | No | Authorised admin only |

### Membership enforcement

The approval process must create a trusted membership record through privileged backend logic.

Only the trusted backend or authorised administrator operations may create, approve, suspend or revoke membership.

A user must never be able to modify their own membership document to become approved.

Update the Firestore rules for the existing Community Hub collection paths so that private chat data requires an approved membership.

The existing channel-specific permissions must remain in force as additional restrictions.

An approved student should still obey existing moderator roles, channel locks and other Community Hub permissions.

A pending or rejected user must not be able to retrieve private chat messages by:

- Opening a direct channel URL.
- Calling Firestore directly.
- Reusing an old local-storage value.
- Editing their browser state.
- Calling a client-side function.
- Modifying their profile document.

Do not trust `localStorage` as evidence of membership.

### Admin authority

Review the existing administrator implementation.

Do not rely solely on a client-side email constant or a user-editable profile role to grant administrative permissions.

Use trusted Firebase Admin SDK custom claims or an equivalent secure server-side admin-authorisation mechanism.

Administrator claims must only be assigned from a privileged trusted environment.

Verify administrative permissions both in the API layer and Firestore rules.

Do not allow users to change their own `role`, `communityAccessStatus` or approval fields.

### Existing user migration

This project already has users and chat history.

Do not delete existing profiles or message data.

Prepare a migration approach for users who registered under the previous system.

Do not automatically assume every old account has verified student status.

Provide an administrative migration or review mechanism so legitimate existing students can retain their access after their records are checked.

Preserve existing admin and moderator privileges through secure migration.

